<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transfer of the Portuguese court to Brazil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as court's flight from Napoleon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The transfer of the Portuguese court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> was the relocation of the Portuguese royal family and government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1808, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s armies invaded Portugal. Rio became the seat of the Portuguese monarchy, and Brazil was raised in status within the realm. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> treat this move as a key foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grito-do-ipiranga">Brazilian independence</a> and the later empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transfer-of-the-portuguese-court-to-brazil.jpg" alt="Transfer of the Portuguese court to Brazil" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas-Louis-Albert Delerive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embarque_da_Fam%C3%ADlia_Real_para_o_Brasil_-_Nicolas-Louis-Albert_Delerive,_attrib._(Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 19 November 1807 French and Spanish forces invaded Portugal, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s last allies on the Continent. Prince Regent John, ruling since 1799 for his mentally ill mother, Queen Maria I, ordered the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> before he could be deposed. Secret negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by the ambassador Domingos António de Sousa Coutinho had secured British protection in exchange for access to Brazil's ports and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a>. The court embarked on 27 November on an Anglo-Portuguese fleet led by the 90-gun Príncipe Real and sailed on 29 November, escorted most of the way by Commodore Graham Moore's ships of the line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The court reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> on 22 January 1808, where John opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s ports to friendly nations, ending the colonial pact that had tied its trade to Portugal. On 1 April 1808 he lifted the ban on manufacturing. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> the crown founded the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, two medical schools, military academies, the Banco do Brasil, a botanical garden, an art academy and an opera house, and built roads between the scattered cities. Between 1808 and 1821 John granted 145 titles of nobility, only six of them to Brazilians, and nearly every official was Portuguese.</p><p>The changes rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. About 328,000 enslaved people arrived during the court's stay and the decade after, and the enslaved came to make up more than half of Rio's population at its height. Fearing revolt after the slave revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, the court created the Royal Police Guard. On 16 December 1815 John raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> to a kingdom equal to Portugal within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>, and after Maria's death in 1816 he became king, acclaimed in Rio in 1818.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Portugal's political crisis after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution of 1820</a> forced John VI to return in April 1821, leaving his heir Pedro in Rio. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> return to colonial status, Pedro refused to leave on the Dia do Fico, 9 January 1822. Brazil declared independence on 7 September 1822, and Pedro was crowned emperor on 12 October. Raised in Rio from the age of nine, he identified as Brazilian, and his position as heir helped him prevent serious Portuguese efforts to retake the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transfer of the Portuguese court to Brazil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pedro, who had grown up in Brazil after the <b>court's flight from Napoleon</b> in 1808, found these terms politically and personally unacceptable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
